\title{How to Prove False Statements: Practical Attacks on Fiat–Shamir}
\taxon{Paper}
\date{2025-01-01}
\meta{external}{https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/118}

\p{
    Dmitry Khovratovich, Ron Rothblum, and Lev Soukhanov. CRYPTO 2025; [ePrint
    2025/118](https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/118).
}

\p{
    \strong{Context.} Historically, the gap between the [random oracle model](dhsorens-004V) and a
    deployed hash function had been known since Canetti, Goldreich, and Halevi showed in 1998 that
    there exist schemes provably secure in the model and insecure under every concrete
    instantiation. Their separations, and the line of work following them, were built by
    diagonalisation: the scheme is constructed so that the statement being proved refers to the hash
    function itself, in the manner of Barak's non-black-box arguments. Because no protocol anyone
    proposed for use looked like that, the field's working position for twenty-five years was that
    the separations were a logical curiosity rather than a warning. Technically, the setting here is
    [Fiat–Shamir](dhsorens-002G) applied to a many-round [public-coin](dhsorens-0053) interactive
    proof, and the property at issue is \em{adaptive} soundness — soundness against a prover that
    chooses its statement after the hash function is fixed. The protocol attacked is the succinct
    argument obtained by applying Fiat–Shamir to the GKR protocol for non-deterministic
    bounded-depth computation, whose verifier cost scales with a circuit's depth rather than its
    size. That efficiency profile is what makes GKR attractive in practice and is also what the
    attack needs: in a GKR argument the statement \em{is} a circuit, so an adaptive prover chooses
    the computation the challenges will be derived about, and a hash function is shallow enough to
    fit inside the statement's depth budget.
}

\p{
    \strong{Contributions.} The paper proves that for every hash function there is an instantiation
    of this standard GKR-based argument whose Fiat–Shamir compilation fails to be adaptively sound,
    and exhibits an explicit circuit together with an accepting proof of a false claim about it. It
    then strengthens this: given any circuit #{C} and any target output #{y}, the authors construct
    a circuit computing the same function as #{C} for which an accepting proof of the claim that it
    outputs #{y} can be produced whether or not that claim is true. The significance of the
    strengthening is that a poisoned circuit is not detectable by evaluating it. The result holds
    for \em{every} hash function rather than for a weak one, so no change of primitive or digest
    length affects it, and it leaves the random oracle proof itself untouched — what it shows is
    that the proof is not a statement about the deployed object.
}

\p{
    \strong{Impact.} The paper ended the position that Fiat–Shamir's standard-model separations were
    confined to contrived protocols, by attaching the phenomenon to one that is textbook and
    deployed. Its immediate effect was to make the adaptivity qualifier mandatory in any Fiat–Shamir
    security claim: a system must now establish whether its statements are fixed before its hash
    function is chosen, which is a question about deployment rather than about cryptography. It
    prompted Arnon and Yogev's [extended Fiat–Shamir transform](dhsorens-004Y) at the same
    conference, designed specifically to block attacks of this shape, which in turn drew a
    counterexample from Kalsta within the year. What it leaves open is the question a system
    operator actually has: whether any fielded prover — a [zkVM](dhsorens-003Q) executing a program
    supplied by an adversary, a verifier accepting a circuit from an untrusted party — admits a
    poisoned instance. The paper establishes that the question is not empty and does not answer it.
}

\p{
    The [technical treatment of the attack](dhsorens-004X) is a separate page; this one covers the
    work's place in the literature. The eprint host is unreachable from cloud sessions, so the
    account above is built from the abstract, from secondary coverage, and from the follow-up
    literature: the theorem statements are reliable, and the construction's internals are described
    only at the level of their shape.
}
